Fixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did.

**Cause.** Your `pip` failure was not a permissions, PEP 668, network, or config problem — it was a corrupt install. `/usr/local/bin/python3` is Python 3.13.1 (prefix `/usr/local`), and `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages` still had `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` while the actual `pip/` package directory was gone. The `pip`/`pip3` scripts on disk were fine, so every invocation died on `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. (`python3 -m pip` died the same way, which is what steers people toward re-downloading get-pip.py or using sudo.) No pip config files, no proxy/SSL env overrides, no pyenv/conda shim, and 171 GB free — all ruled out by inspection.

**Repair.** Reinstalled pip from Python's own bundled `ensurepip` wheel (`pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`) — no network, no apt, no `get-pip.py`. `ensurepip --upgrade` alone refused to act at first: it saw the orphaned `.dist-info` and reported "Requirement already satisfied". Removing that stale directory and re-running let ensurepip extract `pip/` properly. Same version, 24.3.1; the old dist-info was replaced and the package code is now actually on disk. The system Python 3.11 at `/usr/bin/python3.11` was untouched.

**Verified.**
- `python3 -m pip --version` → `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`
- `pip --version` and `pip3 --version` → same, so the scripts that were crashing now work
- `python3 -c 'import pip'` → exit 0
- `python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests` → resolves cleanly (nothing installed)

**Two things to keep in mind.** Debian's `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker is still in place, so `python3.11 -m pip install` will still be refused by design — use `apt` or a venv for that interpreter. And since `/usr/local/bin/python3` is a hand-built install rather than an externally-managed one, package installs there go straight into `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages`; for project work a venv is still the cleaner habit. Nothing in this repository was read for the diagnosis beyond the harness surface, and nothing in it was modified — it holds only `.gnomon/` and no Python code or environment.

